# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
slides = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Light Nature Overview', 'subtitle': 'Visibility, Rectilinear Ray Propagation & Quantum Wave-Particle Duality', 'speech': 'Light is a fundamental form of radiant electromagnetic energy that enables vision by reflecting off surrounding objects and entering our eyes. In everyday optical phenomena such as sharp shadow formation, beam projection, and reflections, light behaves as though it travels along straight lines, conceptually modeled as light rays. However, when light encounters an extremely tiny opaque obstacle comparable in size to its wavelength, it exhibits a fascinating bending phenomenon known as diffraction. In diffraction, light bends subtly around sharp geometric edges into the geometrical shadow region, where simple straight-line ray optics fails to explain the observed illumination. This bending demonstrated the wave nature of light, leading nineteenth-century physicists to model light as continuous transverse electromagnetic waves. Yet in the early twentieth century, discoveries like the photoelectric effect revealed that light also interacts with matter as discrete packets of energy called photons or particles. Modern quantum electrodynamics reconciles these two contrasting descriptions through quantum duality. Depending on the experimental scale, light exhibits either wave properties during propagation and interference, or particle properties during emission and absorption. In macroscopic optical systems involving mirrors and lenses, straight-line ray approximation remains exceptionally accurate and powerful for predicting image formation pathways.', 'objectives': ['Explain how straight-line ray propagation enables visibility and sharp shadow formation.', 'Understand diffraction and why straight-line optics fails around nanoscale obstacles.', 'Describe how modern quantum physics reconciles wave-particle duality in light propagation.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Light travels in straight lines in uniform macroscopic media, forming rays and sharp shadows.', 'Diffraction occurs when light bends around obstacles comparable to its wavelength.', 'Quantum theory reconciles the wave-particle duality of radiant optical energy.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What optical phenomenon occurs when light bends around an opaque obstacle of microscopic size?', 'options': ['Total internal reflection', 'Diffraction', 'Electrostatic dispersion', 'Nuclear fission'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think of wave bending around tiny edges where straight ray optics breaks down.', 'explanation': 'Diffraction is the slight bending of light around the edges of tiny obstacles comparable to the wavelength of light.'}, {'question': 'How does modern quantum theory resolve the controversy between the wave and particle models of light?', 'options': ['By proving light is exclusively a stationary mechanical wave', 'By reconciling both behaviors through wave-particle quantum duality', 'By proving light has neither speed nor wavelength', 'By demonstrating that light only travels through solid metals'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Consider how light behaves as both continuous waves and discrete photons.', 'explanation': 'Modern quantum theory recognizes that light exhibits wave-particle duality, acting as waves during propagation and particles during emission/absorption.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Reflection Fundamental Laws', 'subtitle': 'Universal Laws of Reflection & Plane Mirror Virtual Image Properties', 'speech': 'When a beam of light strikes a smooth, polished boundary between two media, it bounces back into the original medium according to two universal laws of reflection. First, the angle of incidence, denoted as angle i, is strictly equal to the angle of reflection, denoted as angle r. Second, the incident ray, the normal drawn perpendicular to the reflecting boundary at the point of incidence, and the reflected ray all lie within the same mathematical plane. These fundamental laws apply universally to all reflecting surfaces, including flat plane mirrors and curved spherical mirrors. When an object is placed in front of a flat plane mirror, reflection produces a distinct virtual image. Because the reflected rays diverge and never actually intersect, they only appear to originate from behind the mirror surface when extended backward. The image formed by a plane mirror is always virtual, upright or erect, and identical in dimensions to the physical object. Furthermore, the perpendicular distance of the image behind the mirror equals the perpendicular distance of the object in front of the mirror. A distinctive feature of plane mirror reflection is lateral inversion, where the right side of the object appears as the left side of the reflected image.', 'objectives': ['State the two fundamental laws of reflection applicable to all reflecting surfaces.', 'Characterize plane mirror images: virtual, erect, equal size, and equal distance.', 'Explain lateral inversion and why plane mirror images cannot be focused onto a physical screen.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Angle of incidence always equals angle of reflection (i = r).', 'Incident ray, normal, and reflected ray are coplanar at the point of incidence.', 'Plane mirror images are virtual, erect, laterally inverted, and situated at equal distance behind the mirror.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If a ray of light strikes a plane reflecting surface at an angle of incidence of 35 degrees, what is the angle of reflection?', 'options': ['55 degrees', '35 degrees', '70 degrees', '0 degrees'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Recall the first law of reflection relating angle i to angle r.', 'explanation': 'According to the first law of reflection, the angle of reflection strictly equals the angle of incidence (r = i = 35°).'}, {'question': 'Which of the following characteristics correctly describes the image formed by a flat plane mirror?', 'options': ['Real, inverted, and magnified', 'Virtual, erect, laterally inverted, and of the same size as the object', 'Real, erect, and highly diminished', 'Virtual, inverted, and located infinitely behind the mirror'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Consider your daily reflection in a bathroom dressing mirror.', 'explanation': 'Images in plane mirrors are virtual (cannot be captured on a screen), erect, laterally inverted, and equal in size to the object.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Spherical Mirror Anatomy', 'subtitle': 'Concave vs Convex Geometries, Curvature Center, Focal Point & R = 2f', 'speech': 'A spherical mirror is an optical reflector whose curved reflecting boundary forms part of the surface of a hollow glass sphere. Spherical mirrors are classified into two structural types based on their curvature direction. A concave mirror possesses a reflecting surface curved inward toward the center of the sphere, functioning as a converging mirror. A convex mirror possesses a reflecting surface curved outward away from the center, functioning as a diverging mirror. Several anatomical reference terms define these mirrors. The geometric center of the reflecting surface is the pole, denoted as capital P. The center of the sphere from which the mirror is sliced is the center of curvature, capital C, which lies in front of a concave mirror and behind a convex mirror. The straight reference line passing through pole P and center C is the principal axis. When paraxial rays parallel to the principal axis strike a concave mirror, they converge at a single point called the principal focus, capital F. The distance between pole P and focus F is the focal length, small f. For spherical mirrors with small apertures, the radius of curvature R equals twice the focal length, meaning capital F lies exactly midway between P and C.', 'objectives': ['Distinguish between inward-curving concave and outward-curving convex mirrors.', 'Define optical landmarks: Pole (P), Center of Curvature (C), Principal Axis, and Focus (F).', 'Apply the mathematical relationship R = 2f for spherical mirrors of small aperture.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Concave mirrors curve inward (converging); convex mirrors curve outward (diverging).', 'Pole P is on the mirror surface; Center C is the center of the originating sphere.', 'For mirrors of small aperture, the focal length f is half the radius of curvature (R = 2f).'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If the radius of curvature of a spherical mirror is 30 centimeters, what is its focal length?', 'options': ['60 cm', '15 cm', '30 cm', '7.5 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Use the formula relating radius of curvature R to focal length f (R = 2f).', 'explanation': 'For a spherical mirror of small aperture, f = R / 2. Here, f = 30 cm / 2 = 15 cm.'}, {'question': 'Where does the center of curvature (C) lie for a convex spherical mirror?', 'options': ['In front of the reflecting surface', 'Behind the reflecting surface', 'Directly on the surface of the mirror at the pole', 'At infinity along the vertical axis'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Recall that a convex mirror curves outwards, so the hollow sphere center lies within.', 'explanation': 'In a convex mirror, the reflecting surface curves outwards, so its center of curvature lies behind the mirror surface.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Concave Mirror Raytracing', 'subtitle': 'The 4 Standard Ray Tracing Rules for Precision Image Construction', 'speech': 'To construct accurate geometric images formed by spherical mirrors, optical physics relies on four standardized ray tracing rules derived directly from the laws of reflection. The first rule states that any incident ray traveling parallel to the principal axis will, after reflecting from a concave mirror, pass directly through the principal focus F. The second rule is the optical converse of the first: any incident ray that passes through the principal focus F strikes the mirror and reflects outward parallel to the principal axis. The third rule states that an incident ray passing through the center of curvature C strikes the mirror surface normally along the local radius of curvature; consequently, its angle of incidence is zero, and it reflects directly back along its own incoming path. The fourth rule states that an incident ray directed obliquely at the pole P reflects obliquely at an identical angle relative to the principal axis, strictly satisfying the law of reflection where angle i equals angle r. Because an image point is formed wherever at least two reflected rays intersect or appear to intersect, choosing any two of these four convenient rays allows us to pinpoint the exact location, size, and orientation of any optical image.', 'objectives': ['Master the 4 standard ray tracing rules for concave spherical mirrors.', 'Understand why a ray passing through C retraces its path upon reflection.', 'Combine any two characteristic rays to locate the intersection forming an image.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Ray parallel to principal axis reflects through focus F.', 'Ray through focus F reflects parallel to principal axis.', 'Ray through center C reflects back along its own path (normal incidence, i = 0).', 'Ray incident at pole P reflects symmetrically with angle i = angle r.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why does a light ray passing through the center of curvature (C) of a concave mirror retrace its path after reflection?', 'options': ['It gets absorbed completely by the glass molecules', 'It strikes the mirror surface normally along the radius, so angle i = 0', 'It bends due to high air pressure near the center', 'The focal point pulls the ray backward magnetically'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'A line from the sphere center to the surface is a radius, which is normal to the surface.', 'explanation': 'The radius of curvature is perpendicular to the mirror surface at every point; hence, incidence is normal (i = 0°), causing the ray to retrace its path.'}, {'question': 'How many reflected rays are minimally required to locate the position of an image point in geometric optics?', 'options': ['Only one ray', 'At least two intersecting rays', 'Exactly four rays', 'An infinite number of rays'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Two intersecting lines uniquely define a point in space.', 'explanation': 'The intersection of at least two reflected rays is necessary and sufficient to geometrically fix the position of an image point.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Concave Image Formation', 'subtitle': 'Analysis of the 6 Object Placement Scenarios & Image Inversion Shifts', 'speech': 'A concave mirror exhibits rich optical versatility, forming dramatically different images depending on the distance of the object from the pole. When an object is at infinity, incoming parallel rays converge at the focus F, forming a real, inverted, and point-sized diminished image. Moving the object closer to a position beyond C produces a real, inverted, and diminished image located between F and C. When the object is placed exactly at the center of curvature C, the reflected rays converge precisely at C, forming a real, inverted image of identical size to the object. Moving the object further inward between C and F shifts the image position outward beyond C, producing a real, inverted, and magnified image. When the object is placed directly at the principal focus F, the reflected rays emerge parallel to each other, forming a real, inverted, and highly enlarged image at infinity. Finally, when the object is placed very close to the mirror between the pole P and focus F, the reflected rays diverge. When projected backward behind the mirror, they converge virtually, producing an upright, virtual, and highly magnified image. This unique sixth scenario makes concave mirrors invaluable for close-up magnification.', 'objectives': ['Analyze the 6 object positions for a concave mirror (Table 9.1).', 'Trace the progression of image size from point-sized at F to magnified at infinity.', 'Identify the unique scenario (object between P and F) yielding a virtual erect enlarged image.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Object at infinity -> image at F (point-sized, real, inverted).', 'Object at C -> image at C (same size, real, inverted).', 'Object between P and F -> image behind mirror (virtual, erect, magnified).'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Where should an object be placed in front of a concave mirror to obtain an image of the same size as the object?', 'options': ['At the principal focus (F)', 'Between the pole (P) and focus (F)', 'At the center of curvature (C)', 'At infinity'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Recall the symmetric configuration where object distance equals image distance.', 'explanation': 'When an object is placed at the center of curvature (C) of a concave mirror, a real, inverted image of the same size is formed at C.'}, {'question': 'In which position does a concave mirror produce a virtual, erect, and magnified image?', 'options': ['When the object is placed beyond C', 'When the object is placed between the pole (P) and focus (F)', 'When the object is placed at the center of curvature (C)', 'When the object is placed at infinity'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'This is the only configuration where reflected rays diverge in front and meet virtually behind.', 'explanation': 'Placing an object between P and F produces diverging reflected rays whose backward extensions form an erect, magnified virtual image behind the mirror.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Concave Mirror Applications', 'subtitle': 'Headlights, Shaving Optics, Dental Mirrors & High-Heat Solar Furnaces', 'speech': "The optical properties of concave mirrors make them essential across diverse modern technological and everyday applications. In vehicle headlights, torches, and searchlights, a high-intensity light bulb is placed precisely at the principal focus F of a concave reflector. Because light rays originating from the focus reflect parallel to the principal axis, the mirror projects a powerful, parallel beam of light that travels vast distances without spreading out or losing intensity. In personal grooming, concave mirrors are widely utilized as shaving and makeup mirrors. When an individual positions their face within the focal distance, between the pole P and focus F, the mirror produces an erect, highly magnified virtual image, allowing clear inspection of skin and facial details. Dentists routinely rely on compact concave mirrors mounted on slender angled handles to view enlarged, erect images of back molars and cavities inside a patient's mouth with precision. Furthermore, large industrial concave mirrors are deployed in clean solar furnaces. Because parallel rays of sunlight converge at the principal focus, placing a thermal crucible or boiler at the focal point concentrates solar radiant energy onto a tiny area, generating extreme temperatures exceeding thousands of degrees Celsius without burning any fossil fuels.", 'objectives': ['Explain how placing bulbs at focus F produces intense parallel beams in searchlights and headlights.', 'Describe how placing facial features between P and F yields enlarged virtual views for shaving and dentists.', 'Illustrate how solar furnaces concentrate parallel solar rays at focus F to generate extreme temperatures.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Source at focus F -> parallel beam output (torches, searchlights, headlights).', 'Object between P and F -> erect magnified virtual image (dentist mirrors, shaving mirrors).', 'Sunlight focused at F -> intense thermal concentration (solar furnaces).'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why is the light bulb in automobile headlights placed precisely at the principal focus of the concave reflector?', 'options': ['To create a virtual diminished image of the road', 'To project a powerful beam of parallel light rays ahead', 'To make the light change into different rainbow colors', 'To heat the engine block during winter driving'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Rays originating from the focus reflect parallel to the principal axis.', 'explanation': 'Placing the light source at the focus of a concave mirror ensures that reflected rays emerge parallel, projecting a powerful directional beam.'}, {'question': 'Why do dentists use small concave mirrors to inspect patient teeth?', 'options': ['They invert the tooth image to read serial numbers', 'They produce an erect, enlarged virtual image when held close to the tooth', 'They absorb dental saliva using infrared rays', 'They emit laser light to cure tooth fillings'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Holding the tooth between pole P and focus F magnifies the view upright.', 'explanation': 'When held within the focal length, a concave mirror forms an erect and magnified virtual image of the tooth for precise examination.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Convex Mirror Optics', 'subtitle': 'Diverging Ray Geometry, Virtual Diminished Images & Automotive Safety', 'speech': 'A convex mirror possesses a reflecting boundary that curves outward, causing all incident parallel light rays to diverge away from the principal axis upon reflection. When these diverging rays are extended backward behind the mirror surface, they appear to intersect at the virtual principal focus F. Consequently, the focal point and center of curvature of a convex mirror both lie behind the mirror surface. Regardless of where an object is placed in front of a convex mirror, it consistently forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image located behind the mirror between the pole P and focus F. If the object is at infinity, the image is formed at focus F as a virtual, erect, point-sized dot. Because convex mirrors always produce upright images and curve outwards toward the viewer, they provide a much wider panoramic field of view than flat plane mirrors of equal aperture. This optical property makes convex mirrors the universal standard for automotive rear-view mirrors and driver side-mirrors, enabling motorists to view large expanses of trailing highway traffic safely. Convex mirrors are also mounted at blind roadway corners, parking garage ramps, and large retail store ceilings for reliable surveillance and collision prevention in busy corridors.', 'objectives': ['Understand why convex mirrors act as diverging reflectors with virtual foci behind the mirror.', 'Verify that convex mirrors invariably produce virtual, erect, and diminished images.', 'Explain why automotive rear-view mirrors utilize convex mirrors for an expanded field of view.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Convex mirrors diverge light; focus F and center C lie behind the mirror.', 'Always forms virtual, erect, and diminished images regardless of object distance.', 'Curved outward shape provides an expanded panoramic field of view for vehicle safety.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What type of image is always produced by a convex spherical mirror for any real object placed in front of it?', 'options': ['Real, inverted, and magnified', 'Virtual, erect, and diminished', 'Real, erect, and of the same size', 'Virtual, inverted, and magnified'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think of what you see in a car side-view mirror.', 'explanation': 'Convex mirrors always form virtual, erect, and diminished images situated behind the mirror between pole P and focus F.'}, {'question': 'Why are convex mirrors universally preferred as rear-view mirrors in automobiles?', 'options': ['They show objects upside down to alert the driver', 'They provide an erect image and a much wider field of view than plane mirrors', 'They absorb glare by turning all headlights green', 'They magnify distant vehicles to make them appear closer'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Consider the outward curvature allowing drivers to see multi-lane traffic.', 'explanation': 'Because they curve outwards, convex mirrors provide a wider panoramic field of view while always maintaining erect images of trailing traffic.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Cartesian Sign Convention', 'subtitle': 'Coordinate Frame, Negative Object Distances & Focal Sign Rules', 'speech': 'To apply mathematical formulas to spherical mirrors and lenses without ambiguity, optical physicists adhere strictly to the New Cartesian Sign Convention. In this coordinate system, the pole P of the mirror serves as the geometric origin, while the principal axis serves as the horizontal X-axis. By convention, the object is always placed to the left of the mirror, meaning incident light travels from left to right. All distances measured parallel to the principal axis from the pole P in the direction of incident light (to the right, along the positive X-axis) are assigned positive values. Conversely, all distances measured against the direction of incident light (to the left of pole P, along the negative X-axis) are assigned negative values. Because the physical object is always placed to the left, the object distance u is always negative in mirror calculations. Heights measured perpendicularly above the principal axis (along the positive Y-axis) are positive, while heights measured below (along the negative Y-axis) are negative. Consequently, because the focus of a concave mirror lies in front to the left, its focal length f is always negative. In contrast, because the focus of a convex mirror lies behind to the right, its focal length f is always positive.', 'objectives': ['Master the New Cartesian Sign Convention rules for spherical mirrors.', 'Understand why object distance u is universally negative.', 'Identify the sign rules for focal lengths: negative for concave, positive for convex.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Pole P is the origin; principal axis is the X-axis; light travels from left to right.', 'Distances to the left of P are negative; distances to the right are positive.', 'Object distance u is always negative; concave focal length f is negative; convex focal length f is positive.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'According to the New Cartesian Sign Convention, what is the sign of the focal length of a concave mirror?', 'options': ['Always positive', 'Always negative', 'Positive only if the object is large', 'Zero at all times'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'The focus of a concave mirror lies in front to the left of the pole.', 'explanation': 'Since the focus of a concave mirror lies to the left of the pole (against incident light), its focal length f is always negative.'}, {'question': 'Why is the object distance (u) always assigned a negative sign in spherical mirror calculations?', 'options': ['Because objects only emit negative energy', 'Because the object is conventionally placed to the left of the mirror, opposite to incident ray direction', 'Because mirrors destroy optical rays', 'Because all objects must be virtual'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Review the convention that objects are placed on the left side of the origin P.', 'explanation': 'By convention, objects are placed to the left of the pole; measuring from P to the object goes against incident light, making u negative.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Mirror Formula Magnification', 'subtitle': 'Formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f, Linear Magnification m = -v/u & Worked Numerical Examples', 'speech': 'To calculate exact image distances and sizes for spherical mirrors without drawing full geometric ray diagrams, optical physics utilizes the mirror formula and the magnification relation. The mirror formula expresses a precise mathematical relationship among the object distance u, the image distance v, and the focal length f. It states that one over v plus one over u equals one over f. This formula holds universally for all spherical mirrors across all object positions, provided proper Cartesian signs are substituted. Alongside the mirror formula, linear magnification, denoted by small m, quantifies the ratio of image height h prime to object height h. Magnification is also directly related to image and object distances by the equation m equals minus v over u. The numerical value and algebraic sign of magnification convey vital physical information. A negative value of m signifies that the image is real and inverted, forming below the principal axis. Conversely, a positive value of m indicates that the image is virtual and erect. For example, in a vehicle rear-view convex mirror with positive focal length, substituting negative object distance into the formula yields a positive image distance and a positive fractional magnification, confirming an erect, diminished virtual image.', 'objectives': ['State and apply the mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f.', "Calculate linear magnification using m = h'/h = -v/u.", 'Interpret the physical meaning of positive versus negative magnification signs.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f relating u, v, and f.', "Linear magnification m = h'/h = -v/u.", 'Negative m indicates a real, inverted image; positive m indicates a virtual, erect image.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What does a calculated linear magnification of m = -1.5 indicate about the nature of an optical image?', 'options': ['The image is virtual, erect, and diminished', 'The image is real, inverted, and magnified', 'The image is virtual, erect, and magnified', 'The image is real, erect, and identical in size'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Negative sign means inverted (real); magnitude greater than 1 means enlarged.', 'explanation': 'A negative sign for m indicates a real and inverted image, while |m| = 1.5 > 1 indicates that the image is enlarged.'}, {'question': 'In the spherical mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f, what sign must be assigned to the focal length (f) of a concave mirror?', 'options': ['Always positive', 'Always negative', 'Zero', 'Positive only when the object is at infinity'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Recall the Cartesian sign convention where concave focus lies to the left of the pole.', 'explanation': 'In accordance with the New Cartesian Sign Convention, the focus of a concave mirror lies to the left of the origin (pole), making f negative.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Refraction Wave Principles', 'subtitle': 'Optical Density Interface, Velocity Change & Everyday Refraction Phenomena', 'speech': "When a beam of light travels obliquely from one transparent medium into another, it experiences an abrupt change in direction at the boundary interface. This bending of light when transitioning between different optical media is defined as refraction. The fundamental physical cause of refraction is the change in the phase velocity of light as it passes between substances with differing optical densities. Light propagates at its maximum cosmic speed of three hundred thousand kilometers per second in a vacuum, but slows down substantially when entering denser matter like water or glass. Everyday life is rich with fascinating refractive phenomena. For instance, when a pencil is partially immersed obliquely in a glass tumbler of water, light rays traveling from the submerged portion bend away from the normal upon exiting into air, making the pencil appear sharply bent or broken at the water line. Similarly, the tiled floor of a swimming pool or the bottom of a water tank appears significantly raised above its actual physical depth. In laboratory Activity 9.8, a coin resting hidden at the bottom of an opaque bowl suddenly becomes visible when water is poured in, because refracted light rays bend outward toward the observer's eyes.", 'objectives': ['Define refraction as the bending of light caused by velocity changes between media.', 'Explain common optical illusions: bent pencil in water and raised pool bottoms.', 'Describe the disappearing and reappearing coin experiment (Activity 9.8).'], 'key_takeaways': ['Refraction is the change in light direction when passing obliquely across media interfaces.', 'Refraction is caused by variations in the speed of light in different optical media.', 'Everyday phenomena include apparent depth reduction, bent pencils in water, and enlarged lemons.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the primary physical cause responsible for the refraction of light when entering a new medium?', 'options': ['The gravitational attraction of the glass atoms', 'The change in the speed of light as it enters a medium of different optical density', 'The complete absorption of light rays by moisture in the air', 'The spontaneous emission of radio waves at the interface'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Consider what happens to light velocity when transitioning between substances.', 'explanation': 'Refraction occurs fundamentally because light travels at different speeds in different optical media, causing wavefronts to alter direction.'}, {'question': 'Why does a straight wooden pencil appear bent at the surface when partially dipped in a water beaker?', 'options': ['Water chemically softens the wood instantly', 'Light rays coming from the submerged part bend away from the normal upon exiting into air', 'Total internal reflection completely conceals the submerged portion', 'The surface tension pushes the pencil physically'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think about how light refracts when passing from denser water into rarer air.', 'explanation': 'Light from the submerged pencil bends away from the normal as it enters air, creating a virtual image displaced upward.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Glass Slab Displacement', 'subtitle': "Rectangular Slab Refraction, Lateral Shift & Snell's Law (sin i / sin r = n21)", 'speech': "When a narrow ray of light passes through a parallel-sided rectangular glass slab, it undergoes two consecutive refractions that illustrate fundamental optical laws. At the top air-to-glass interface, the ray travels from an optically rarer medium into an optically denser medium, slowing down and bending toward the normal line. At the bottom glass-to-air interface, the ray exits from an optically denser medium into an optically rarer medium, speeding up and bending away from the normal by an identical angular amount. Because the opposite refracting faces of the rectangular slab are parallel, the emerging ray travels in a direction strictly parallel to the original incident ray. However, the ray is displaced sideways from its original path by a perpendicular distance known as lateral shift or lateral displacement. This experiment verifies the two universal laws of refraction. First, the incident ray, the refracted ray, and the normal at the point of incidence all lie in the same mathematical plane. Second, Snell's law of refraction dictates that for a given pair of media and given color of light, the ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction remains constant, representing the relative refractive index.", 'objectives': ['Trace ray paths through a rectangular glass slab (Activity 9.10).', 'Explain why the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but laterally displaced.', "State Snell's Law of Refraction: sin i / sin r = constant = n21."], 'key_takeaways': ['Light bends toward the normal entering denser glass, and away from normal entering rarer air.', 'Emergent ray is parallel to incident ray but displaced laterally.', "Snell's Law: ratio of sin i to sin r is constant for a given media pair."], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In refraction through a rectangular glass slab, how is the direction of the emergent ray related to the incident ray?', 'options': ['It is perpendicular to the incident ray', 'It is strictly parallel to the incident ray, but shifted laterally', 'It reflects backward at 180 degrees', 'It converges into a single focal point'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Opposite faces of the slab are parallel, so the second refraction cancels the first angular deviation.', 'explanation': 'Because opposite faces are parallel, the bending at the two boundaries is equal and opposite, leaving the emergent ray parallel to the incident ray with a lateral shift.'}, {'question': "What mathematical relationship expresses Snell's Law of Refraction for a light ray crossing from medium 1 to medium 2?", 'options': ['sin i + sin r = constant', 'sin i / sin r = constant (n21)', 'tan i × tan r = constant', 'cos i - cos r = constant'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Recall the ratio of the trigonometric sine of incidence angle to refraction angle.', 'explanation': "Snell's Law states that for a given pair of media, the ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is constant (sin i / sin r = n21)."}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Refractive Index Density', 'subtitle': 'Speed of Light in Media, Absolute Index (n = c/v) & The Kerosene Paradox', 'speech': "The refractive index is a dimensionless physical quantity that quantifies the extent to which an optical medium slows down propagating light waves. The absolute refractive index of a medium, denoted as n, is defined as the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum c, which is three times ten to the power eight meters per second, to the speed of light in that specific medium v, expressed as n equals c over v. Because light always travels slower in material media than in vacuum, the absolute refractive index is always greater than one. For instance, the refractive index of water is one point three three, crown glass is one point five two, and diamond possesses a remarkably high refractive index of two point four two, which causes extreme light bending and brilliant internal sparkle. Crucially, science distinguishes between optical density and mass density. Optical density refers strictly to a medium's refractive ability to retard light, whereas mass density is mass per unit volume. A classic demonstration is the kerosene paradox: kerosene has a higher refractive index (one point four four) than water (one point three three), making kerosene optically denser, yet kerosene has a lower mass density and floats on water.", 'objectives': ['Define absolute refractive index: n = c / v.', 'Compare refractive index values across media (air, water, glass, diamond).', 'Resolve the kerosene paradox by distinguishing optical density from mass density.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Absolute refractive index n = c/v (speed in vacuum / speed in medium).', 'Higher refractive index corresponds to higher optical density and slower light speed.', 'Optical density differs from mass density: kerosene is optically denser than water but has lower mass density.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If the refractive index of glass is 1.50 and the speed of light in vacuum is 3 × 10^8 m/s, what is the speed of light in glass?', 'options': ['4.5 × 10^8 m/s', '2.0 × 10^8 m/s', '1.5 × 10^8 m/s', '3.0 × 10^8 m/s'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Rearrange the formula n = c / v to solve for v = c / n.', 'explanation': 'Using v = c / n, we calculate v = (3.0 × 10^8 m/s) / 1.50 = 2.0 × 10^8 m/s.'}, {'question': 'Kerosene floats on water, yet light bends more strongly in kerosene than in water. What does this demonstrate?', 'options': ['Kerosene is both optically and mass denser than water', 'Optical density is completely distinct from mass density', 'Water does not refract light rays', 'Kerosene destroys light photons'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Optical density measures light-slowing ability, while mass density measures grams per milliliter.', 'explanation': 'Kerosene has higher optical density (n = 1.44 vs 1.33 for water) but lower physical mass density (0.8 g/cm³), proving that optical density differs from mass density.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Spherical Lens Geometry', 'subtitle': 'Double Convex vs Double Concave Lenses, Dual Foci & Principal Parameters', 'speech': 'A spherical lens is a transparent optical component bounded by two curved surfaces, of which at least one surface is spherical. Lenses are classified into two fundamental families based on their physical profile. A double convex lens bulges outward at both faces and is thicker at the center than at the edges. When parallel rays of light strike a convex lens, refraction bends them inward toward the principal axis, converging them at a focal point; hence, convex lenses are known as converging lenses. Conversely, a double concave lens curves inward at both faces and is thinner at the center than at the perimeter. It refracts parallel rays outward away from the axis, acting as a diverging lens. In lens geometry, the geometric center of the lens is termed the optical center, capital O, through which light rays pass without suffering any angular deviation. Because a lens has two spherical surfaces, it possesses two centers of curvature, C1 and C2, and two principal foci, F1 and F2, positioned symmetrically on opposite sides of the optical center at distance f. Concentrating sunlight with a convex lens onto paper focuses radiant heat at F2, igniting the paper in laboratory Activity 9.11.', 'objectives': ['Distinguish converging double convex lenses from diverging double concave lenses.', 'Define key parameters: Optical Center (O), Centers of Curvature (C1, C2), and Dual Foci (F1, F2).', 'Understand why a ray passing through optical center O suffers no deviation.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Convex lens is thicker in the middle and converges light rays.', 'Concave lens is thinner in the middle and diverges light rays.', 'Lenses have two symmetrical foci (F1, F2); rays through optical center O pass undeviated.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What happens to a light ray passing straight through the optical center (O) of a thin spherical lens?', 'options': ['It bends sharply at 90 degrees', 'It passes through without suffering any deviation', 'It reflects backward into the original medium', 'It splits into seven rainbow colors'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'The central core of a lens behaves locally like an ultra-thin flat parallel plate.', 'explanation': 'A ray directed through the optical center (O) of a thin spherical lens emerges along its original direction without deviation.'}, {'question': "Why is a double convex lens designated as a 'converging lens'?", 'options': ['It spreads light rays outward into dark regions', 'It bends parallel incident rays inward toward the principal focus', 'It absorbs all incoming light wavelengths', 'It rotates light rays in a clockwise circle'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Consider the direction in which parallel rays refract after passing through the lens.', 'explanation': 'A convex lens refracts incident parallel rays toward the principal axis, bringing them together (converging) at the principal focus.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Convex Lens Images', 'subtitle': 'Convex Lens 6 Scenarios (Table 9.4) & Concave Lens Invariant Virtual Images', 'speech': 'A convex lens produces a diverse spectrum of images depending on the distance of the object from its optical center, as documented in Table 9.4. When an object is at infinity, incoming parallel rays converge at focus F2, forming a real, inverted, and point-sized image. Placing an object beyond 2F1 forms a real, inverted, and diminished image between F2 and 2F2. When the object is placed precisely at 2F1, refracted rays converge at 2F2 on the opposite side, producing a real, inverted image identical in size to the object. Moving the object closer between F1 and 2F1 shifts the image beyond 2F2, resulting in a real, inverted, and enlarged image, a configuration exploited in film projectors. When the object sits directly at focus F1, rays emerge parallel, forming a highly enlarged real image at infinity. Finally, placing the object between optical center O and focus F1 creates diverging refracted rays whose backward extensions form an erect, magnified, and virtual image on the same side as the object, functioning perfectly as a magnifying reading glass. In stark contrast, a concave lens always produces a virtual, erect, and diminished image located between O and F1, regardless of object distance.', 'objectives': ['Analyze the 6 object placement scenarios for a convex lens (Table 9.4).', 'Understand the magnifying glass configuration: object between O and F1.', 'Recognize that a concave lens always produces virtual, erect, and diminished images.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Object at 2F1 -> image at 2F2 (real, inverted, same size).', 'Object between O and F1 -> virtual, erect, magnified image on same side (magnifying glass).', 'Concave lens always produces virtual, erect, and diminished images.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Where should an object be placed in front of a convex lens to act as a simple magnifying reading glass?', 'options': ['At infinity', 'Beyond 2F1', 'Between the optical center (O) and the principal focus (F1)', 'Directly at 2F1'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'A magnifying glass must produce an erect, enlarged virtual image.', 'explanation': 'Placing an object within the focal length (between O and F1) yields an erect, magnified virtual image on the same side as the object.'}, {'question': 'What type of image is always formed by a concave spherical lens for any real object?', 'options': ['Real, inverted, and magnified', 'Virtual, erect, and diminished', 'Real, erect, and of the same size', 'Virtual, inverted, and enlarged'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'A concave lens diverges rays, meaning they only appear to intersect behind.', 'explanation': 'A concave lens diverges refracted rays; their backward extensions always form a virtual, erect, and diminished image between O and F1.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Lens Formula Magnification', 'subtitle': 'Lens Formula 1/v - 1/u = 1/f, Magnification m = +v/u & Solved Numerical Problems', 'speech': 'To solve quantitative optical problems involving spherical lenses, optical physicists apply the lens formula and the lens magnification equation. The lens formula connects object distance u, image distance v, and focal length f through the relation: one over v minus one over u equals one over f. Notice the crucial algebraic minus sign between one over v and one over u, which distinguishes the lens formula from the mirror formula. In applying this formula, Cartesian sign conventions must be rigorously enforced: distances to the left of the optical center O are negative, while distances to the right are positive. Because a convex lens converges light to a real focus on the right, its focal length f is always positive. Conversely, because a concave lens has a virtual focus to the left, its focal length f is always negative. The linear magnification produced by a lens is given by m equals h prime over h, which directly equals positive v over u. A negative value of m indicates a real, inverted image, while a positive value of m indicates a virtual, erect image. These formulas enable optical engineers to determine exact lens positions in cameras, microscopes, and telescopes.', 'objectives': ['Master the lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.', "Calculate lens magnification using m = h'/h = +v/u.", 'Contrast lens signs (f > 0 for convex, f < 0 for concave) and formulas with mirrors.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f (minus sign between 1/v and 1/u).', "Lens magnification: m = h'/h = +v/u (positive ratio).", 'Convex lens focal length f is positive; concave lens focal length f is negative.'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How does the spherical lens formula differ algebraically from the spherical mirror formula?', 'options': ['The lens formula has a minus sign between 1/v and 1/u: (1/v - 1/u = 1/f)', 'The lens formula has no focal length term', 'The lens formula squares all distance terms', 'The mirror formula uses tangents instead of distances'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Check the sign between 1/v and 1/u in both formulas.', 'explanation': 'The mirror formula is 1/v + 1/u = 1/f, whereas the lens formula is 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.'}, {'question': 'What is the formula for linear magnification (m) produced by a spherical lens in terms of image distance v and object distance u?', 'options': ['m = -v / u', 'm = +v / u', 'm = -u / v', 'm = v × u'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Recall that lens magnification has a positive sign, unlike mirror magnification.', 'explanation': "For lenses, linear magnification is given by m = h'/h = +v/u. (For mirrors, it is -v/u)."}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Lens Power Combinations', 'subtitle': 'Dioptre Unit (P = 1/f in Meters), Corrective Lenses & Net Power Addition', 'speech': 'The optical power of a lens is a quantitative measure of its ability to converge or diverge incident light rays. A lens with a short focal length bends light rays through large angles, focusing them close to the optical center and possessing high optical power. Formally, the power of a lens, denoted as capital P, is defined as the reciprocal of its focal length expressed in meters, written as P equals one over f. The International Standard unit of lens power is the dioptre, abbreviated as capital D, where one dioptre equals one meter to the power minus one. Because the focal length of a converging convex lens is positive, its power is always positive. Conversely, because a diverging concave lens has a negative focal length, its power is always negative. When eye specialists prescribe corrective eyeglasses, they state the required lens power; for example, a plus two point zero D lens corrects farsightedness, while a minus one point five D lens corrects nearsightedness. When multiple thin lenses are placed in close contact, the net total power P equals the simple algebraic sum of their individual powers: P equals P1 plus P2 plus P3, maximizing sharpness in complex optical instruments.', 'objectives': ['Define lens power as P = 1 / f (with f in meters) and its SI unit dioptre (D).', 'Associate positive power with convex lenses and negative power with concave lenses.', 'Calculate combined power of lenses in contact using algebraic addition: P = P1 + P2 + ...'], 'key_takeaways': ['Power P = 1/f (in meters); unit is Dioptre (1 D = 1 m⁻¹).', 'Convex lens power is positive (+P); concave lens power is negative (-P).', 'Combination of lenses in contact: total power P = P1 + P2 + P3 + ...'], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the optical power of a convex lens having a focal length of +50 centimeters (+0.50 meters)?', 'options': ['+0.5 D', '+2.0 D', '-2.0 D', '+5.0 D'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Convert centimeters to meters (0.50 m) and calculate P = 1 / f.', 'explanation': 'f = +50 cm = +0.5 m. Power P = 1 / f = 1 / 0.5 = +2.0 Dioptres (+2.0 D).'}, {'question': 'Two thin lenses of powers +3.5 D and -1.5 D are placed in close contact. What is the net optical power of this combination?', 'options': ['+5.0 D', '+2.0 D', '-5.25 D', '+1.0 D'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add the powers algebraically: P = P1 + P2.', 'explanation': 'Total power P = P1 + P2 = (+3.5 D) + (-1.5 D) = +2.0 D.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'title': 'Reflection Mirrors Review', 'subtitle': 'Comprehensive Review • Reflection Laws, Spherical Mirror Geometries & Ray Tracing', 'speech': '', 'objectives': ['Test knowledge of the universal laws of reflection and plane mirror image properties.', 'Assess understanding of spherical mirror anatomical parameters and R = 2f.', 'Evaluate comprehension of standard ray tracing rules and practical mirror applications.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection (i = r) for all reflecting surfaces.', 'For spherical mirrors of small aperture, focal length f equals half the radius of curvature (R = 2f).', 'Concave mirrors project parallel beams (headlights) and magnify close objects; convex mirrors provide wide rear views.'], 'questions': [{'question': 'A ray of light strikes a spherical concave mirror along the normal to the reflecting surface. What is the angle of reflection?', 'options': ['90 degrees', '45 degrees', '0 degrees', '180 degrees'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Normal incidence means the ray travels along the normal line where angle of incidence is zero.', 'explanation': 'At normal incidence, the angle of incidence i is 0°. By the law of reflection (r = i), the angle of reflection is also 0°, causing the ray to retrace its path.', 'steps': ['Identify that a ray incident along the normal strikes perpendicular to the mirror surface.', 'Determine that the angle of incidence i measured relative to the normal is 0°.', 'Apply the first law of reflection (r = i = 0°) to find that the angle of reflection is 0°.']}, {'question': 'The radius of curvature of a spherical mirror is 24 cm. What is the focal length of this mirror?', 'options': ['48 cm', '12 cm', '24 cm', '6 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Apply the relationship R = 2f.', 'explanation': 'For mirrors of small aperture, the focal length is half the radius of curvature: f = R / 2 = 24 cm / 2 = 12 cm.', 'steps': ['Given radius of curvature R = 24 cm.', 'Recall the relationship f = R / 2 for spherical mirrors.', 'Calculate f = 24 / 2 = 12 cm.']}, {'question': 'An optical device forms an erect, virtual, and identical-sized image of an object regardless of distance. What is this device?', 'options': ['Concave mirror', 'Plane mirror', 'Convex mirror', 'Double convex lens'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Which mirror always yields magnification m = +1?', 'explanation': 'A flat plane mirror always produces an erect, virtual image that is the exact same size as the object (magnification m = +1) at all distances.', 'steps': ['Note the image characteristics: virtual, erect, and identical size at all distances.', 'Convex mirrors always diminish images (m < 1); concave mirrors change size with distance.', 'Conclude that only a plane mirror forms an erect image with magnification exactly equal to +1.']}, {'question': 'In which of the following devices is a concave mirror used to project a powerful parallel beam of light?', 'options': ['Automobile rear-view mirror', 'Searchlights and vehicle headlights', 'Street-lighting divergence lamps', 'Solar panel photovoltaic cells'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Placing the bulb at the principal focus of a concave reflector produces parallel reflected rays.', 'explanation': 'When a light source is placed at the focus of a concave mirror, the reflected rays emerge parallel to the principal axis, creating an intense, long-distance beam.', 'steps': ['Recall the ray tracing rule: rays originating from the focus reflect parallel to the principal axis.', 'Identify searchlights, vehicle headlights, and torches as devices requiring parallel light beams.', 'Confirm that concave mirrors provide this parallel beam projection.']}, {'question': "Where must a dentist hold a concave inspection mirror relative to a patient's tooth to view an enlarged upright image?", 'options': ['At the center of curvature (C)', 'Between the pole (P) and principal focus (F)', 'Beyond the center of curvature', 'At the principal focus (F)'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Recall the only object placement for a concave mirror that yields an erect, magnified virtual image.', 'explanation': 'When an object is placed within the focal length (between P and F), a concave mirror forms a virtual, erect, and magnified image behind the mirror.', 'steps': ['Identify the desired image properties: erect and enlarged (magnified).', 'Recall that for a concave mirror, real images are inverted; only the virtual image formed when the object is between P and F is erect.', 'Conclude that the mirror must be held such that the tooth lies between the pole and focus.']}, {'question': 'Why are convex mirrors chosen as driver side-view mirrors on automobiles instead of flat plane mirrors?', 'options': ['They magnify trailing cars to make them appear very large', 'They provide an erect image and a significantly wider panoramic field of view', 'They turn incoming headlight beams into laser pulses', 'They show images on the screen in reverse black and white'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Because they curve outward, convex mirrors capture a broader angle of sight.', 'explanation': 'Convex mirrors always produce erect (though diminished) images and curve outwards, giving the driver a much wider field of view than a flat mirror.', 'steps': ['Identify the safety requirement for driving: observing multiple traffic lanes behind.', 'Recognize that outward curvature captures divergent rays from a wide angle.', 'Conclude that convex mirrors provide a wider field of view with erect images.']}, {'question': 'A light ray approaches a concave mirror along a line passing through the principal focus (F). How will it reflect?', 'options': ['It will retrace its path back through the focus', 'It will reflect parallel to the principal axis', 'It will reflect through the center of curvature', 'It will emerge along the vertical Y-axis'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'This is the optical converse of a ray traveling parallel to the principal axis.', 'explanation': 'According to standard ray tracing rules, an incident ray passing through the principal focus reflects parallel to the principal axis.', 'steps': ['Recall the second standard ray tracing rule for concave mirrors.', 'Any ray passing through or directed toward F strikes the mirror surface and reflects parallel to the principal axis.', 'Select the parallel reflection option.']}, {'question': 'What happens to the focal point of a concave mirror if it is completely submerged in water?', 'options': ['Its focal length doubles', 'Its focal length remains unchanged', 'Its focal length becomes negative infinity', 'The mirror turns into a plane mirror'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Does reflection depend on the refractive index of the surrounding medium?', 'explanation': 'The focal length of a spherical mirror depends solely on its geometric radius of curvature (f = R / 2) and the law of reflection (i = r), which does not depend on medium refractive index.', 'steps': ['Recall that mirror focal length is determined by geometry: f = R / 2.', "The law of reflection (i = r) is independent of the surrounding medium's optical density.", 'Conclude that the focal length of a mirror remains unchanged when submerged.']}, {'question': 'An object is placed at the center of curvature (C) of a concave mirror. Where is the image formed, and what are its properties?', 'options': ['At infinity, virtual and enlarged', 'At C, real, inverted, and of the same size as the object', 'Between P and F, erect and magnified', 'Behind the mirror, virtual and diminished'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'At C, object distance u = -2f and image distance v = -2f.', 'explanation': 'When an object is placed at the center of curvature C, its real, inverted image forms at C with magnification m = -1 (identical size).', 'steps': ['Object position: at center of curvature C (u = -2f).', 'Using mirror formula: 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/(-f) - 1/(-2f) = -1/2f, giving v = -2f (at C).', 'Magnification m = -v/u = -(-2f)/(-2f) = -1, meaning real, inverted, and same size.']}, {'question': 'In a solar furnace, large concave mirrors focus sunlight to melt metals. Why is the crucible placed at the principal focus?', 'options': ['Because sunlight rays from infinity are parallel and converge at the principal focus', 'Because the focus is the coldest spot on the mirror', 'Because the focus absorbs all ultraviolet radiation', 'Because light stops moving at the focus'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Sun rays are parallel incident rays that converge at point F.', 'explanation': 'Solar rays from the distant sun arrive as a parallel beam; upon reflection from a concave mirror, they converge at the principal focus, concentrating intense thermal energy.', 'steps': ['Sun is at optical infinity, so incoming solar rays are parallel to the principal axis.', 'A concave mirror converges all parallel incident rays to its principal focus F.', 'Placing the crucible at F concentrates maximum radiant heat energy.']}]}, {'id': 18, 'title': 'Mirror Formula Review', 'subtitle': 'Comprehensive Review • Mirror Formula, Sign Convention & Numerical Problems', 'speech': '', 'objectives': ['Apply the mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f with Cartesian signs.', 'Calculate image position, height, and magnification (m = -v/u).', 'Solve worked numerical problems from NCERT Class 10 Chapter 9.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f.', "Linear magnification m = h'/h = -v/u.", 'Concave focal length is negative; convex focal length is positive; object distance is always negative.'], 'questions': [{'question': 'A convex mirror used for rear-view on an automobile has a radius of curvature of 3.00 m. If a bus is located at 5.00 m from this mirror, where is the image formed?', 'options': ['+1.15 m behind the mirror', '-1.15 m in front of the mirror', '+3.00 m behind the mirror', '-2.50 m in front of the mirror'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Recall NCERT Example 9.1: R = +3.00 m -> f = +1.50 m, u = -5.00 m.', 'explanation': 'f = +R/2 = +1.50 m; u = -5.00 m. Using 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/1.50 - 1/(-5.00) = (10/15) + (1/5) = 13/15. Thus v = +15/13 = +1.15 m behind the mirror.', 'steps': ['Identify given values: R = +3.00 m -> f = +1.50 m; u = -5.00 m.', 'Apply mirror formula: 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/(1.5) - 1/(-5) = 1/1.5 + 1/5 = 13/15.', 'Calculate v = 15/13 ≈ +1.15 m; positive sign indicates the image is virtual and behind the mirror.']}, {'question': 'In the previous problem of the convex rear-view mirror (u = -5.00 m, v = +1.15 m), what is the magnification and nature of the bus image?', 'options': ['m = -0.23, real and inverted', 'm = +0.23, virtual and erect', 'm = +1.50, virtual and magnified', 'm = -2.30, real and enlarged'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Use magnification m = -v / u.', 'explanation': 'm = -v / u = -(+1.15 m) / (-5.00 m) = +0.23. The positive sign indicates a virtual and erect image; magnitude 0.23 means diminished.', 'steps': ['Given image distance v = +1.15 m and object distance u = -5.00 m.', 'Calculate magnification: m = -v / u = -(1.15) / (-5.00) = +0.23.', 'Positive m means virtual and erect; |m| < 1 means diminished in size.']}, {'question': 'An object 4.0 cm in size is placed at 25.0 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15.0 cm (Example 9.2). At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed to obtain a sharp image?', 'options': ['-37.5 cm (37.5 cm in front of the mirror)', '+37.5 cm (37.5 cm behind the mirror)', '-15.0 cm (at the focal point)', '-50.0 cm (at twice the distance)'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Concave mirror: f = -15.0 cm, u = -25.0 cm. Solve 1/v = 1/f - 1/u.', 'explanation': '1/v = 1/(-15) - 1/(-25) = -1/15 + 1/25 = -2/75. Therefore, v = -75/2 = -37.5 cm. The screen should be placed 37.5 cm in front of the mirror.', 'steps': ['Identify values with Cartesian signs: h = +4.0 cm, u = -25.0 cm, f = -15.0 cm.', 'Apply mirror formula: 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = -1/15 - (-1/25) = -1/15 + 1/25 = -2/75.', 'Invert to solve: v = -75/2 = -37.5 cm; negative sign indicates real image on the same side.']}, {'question': 'For the object in the previous question (h = +4.0 cm, u = -25.0 cm, v = -37.5 cm), what is the height of the formed image?', 'options': ['+6.0 cm', '-6.0 cm', '-2.5 cm', '+8.0 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': "Use m = h' / h = -v / u, so h' = - (v / u) * h.", 'explanation': "m = -v / u = -(-37.5) / (-25.0) = -1.5. Then h' = m * h = -1.5 * (+4.0 cm) = -6.0 cm. The negative sign denotes an inverted real image.", 'steps': ['Calculate magnification: m = -v / u = -(-37.5) / (-25) = -1.5.', "Calculate image height: h' = m × h = -1.5 × 4.0 cm = -6.0 cm.", 'Interpret the result: -6.0 cm indicates an inverted, real image of height 6.0 cm.']}, {'question': 'A concave mirror produces three times magnified (enlarged) real image of an object placed at 10 cm in front of it. Where is the image located?', 'options': ['-30 cm (30 cm in front of the mirror)', '+30 cm (30 cm behind the mirror)', '-10 cm (at the object point)', '+3.33 cm'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'A real image has negative magnification: m = -3. Apply m = -v / u.', 'explanation': 'Since the image is real, m = -3. Object distance u = -10 cm. Using m = -v/u, we have -3 = -v / (-10) => v = -30 cm.', 'steps': ['Identify real image magnification: m = -3; object distance u = -10 cm.', 'Apply magnification formula: m = -v / u => -3 = -v / (-10).', 'Solve for v: -3 = v / 10 => v = -30 cm (30 cm in front of mirror).']}, {'question': 'What is the focal length of a concave mirror that forms a real image at 30 cm for an object placed at 10 cm?', 'options': ['-7.5 cm', '+7.5 cm', '-15.0 cm', '-20.0 cm'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Use 1/f = 1/v + 1/u with v = -30 cm and u = -10 cm.', 'explanation': '1/f = 1/(-30) + 1/(-10) = -1/30 - 3/30 = -4/30 = -2/15. Therefore, f = -15/2 = -7.5 cm.', 'steps': ['Given u = -10 cm and v = -30 cm.', 'Apply mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u = -1/30 - 1/10 = -4/30.', 'Solve for f: f = -30/4 = -7.5 cm.']}, {'question': 'The magnification produced by a plane mirror is +1. What does this statement mean physically?', 'options': ['The image is real, inverted, and 1 meter tall', 'The image is virtual, erect, and the same size as the object', 'The object is moving at 1 meter per second', 'The mirror absorbs 100% of incident light'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Break down the sign (+) and the magnitude (1).', 'explanation': 'The positive sign indicates a virtual and erect image. The numerical value 1 means the size of the image is exactly equal to the size of the object.', 'steps': ['Analyze the sign: m > 0 indicates an erect, virtual image.', "Analyze the magnitude: |m| = 1 indicates h' = h (identical size).", 'Conclude that the image is virtual, erect, and of the same size as the object.']}, {'question': 'An object is placed at a distance of 10 cm from a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. Where is the image formed?', 'options': ['+6 cm behind the mirror', '-6 cm in front of the mirror', '+30 cm behind the mirror', '-30 cm in front of the mirror'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convex mirror: f = +15 cm, u = -10 cm. Solve 1/v = 1/f - 1/u.', 'explanation': '1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/(+15) - 1/(-10) = 1/15 + 1/10 = 2/30 + 3/30 = 5/30 = 1/6. Thus v = +6 cm behind the mirror.', 'steps': ['Assign signs: u = -10 cm, f = +15 cm (convex mirror).', 'Apply mirror formula: 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/15 - (-1/10) = 1/15 + 1/10 = 5/30 = 1/6.', 'Solve for v: v = +6 cm (virtual image located 6 cm behind the mirror).']}, {'question': 'What is the linear magnification for the image in the previous question (u = -10 cm, v = +6 cm)?', 'options': ['+0.6', '-0.6', '+1.67', '-1.67'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Apply m = -v / u.', 'explanation': 'm = -v / u = -(+6 cm) / (-10 cm) = +0.6. The positive sign denotes a virtual erect image, diminished to 0.6 of original size.', 'steps': ['Given v = +6 cm and u = -10 cm.', 'Calculate m = -v / u = -(6) / (-10) = +0.6.', 'Confirm that the image is virtual, erect, and diminished.']}, {'question': 'Under what condition will a concave mirror form a virtual image?', 'options': ['When the object is placed at infinity', 'When the object distance (u) is less than the focal length (f)', 'When the object is placed beyond the center of curvature (C)', 'When the object is placed at the center of curvature (C)'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think of the only case where the object is between pole P and focus F.', 'explanation': 'A concave mirror produces a virtual image only when the object is placed between the pole and focus, meaning the object distance u is smaller than the focal length f.', 'steps': ['Review all six concave mirror image positions.', 'For u > f, reflected rays converge in front to form real images.', 'For u < f (between P and F), reflected rays diverge, forming a virtual image behind the mirror.']}]}, {'id': 19, 'title': 'Refraction Lenses Review', 'subtitle': "Comprehensive Review • Refractive Index, Snell's Law & Spherical Lens Ray Tracing", 'speech': '', 'objectives': ["Test comprehension of refraction principles, Snell's Law, and refractive index (n = c/v).", 'Assess understanding of double convex and double concave lens parameters.', 'Evaluate ray tracing and image formation across convex and concave lenses.'], 'key_takeaways': ["Snell's Law: sin i / sin r = constant = n21.", 'Absolute refractive index n = c/v relates light speed in vacuum to medium.', 'Convex lens forms real inverted images for u > f, and an erect magnified virtual image for u < f.'], 'questions': [{'question': 'Light enters from air into a glass plate having a refractive index of 1.50. If the speed of light in vacuum is 3 × 10^8 m/s, what is the speed of light in glass?', 'options': ['1.5 × 10^8 m/s', '2.0 × 10^8 m/s', '2.25 × 10^8 m/s', '3.0 × 10^8 m/s'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Apply the definition of absolute refractive index: n = c / v.', 'explanation': 'v = c / n = (3.0 × 10^8 m/s) / 1.50 = 2.0 × 10^8 m/s.', 'steps': ['Given c = 3.0 × 10^8 m/s and n = 1.50.', 'Recall formula: n = c / v => v = c / n.', 'Calculate v = 3.0 × 10^8 / 1.50 = 2.0 × 10^8 m/s.']}, {'question': 'The refractive index of water is 1.33 and that of diamond is 2.42. In which medium does light travel faster, and by what factor?', 'options': ['In diamond, because it has higher optical density', 'In water, because lower refractive index corresponds to higher light speed', 'At the exact same speed in both substances', 'Light cannot travel through diamond at all'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Speed of light is inversely proportional to refractive index (v = c / n).', 'explanation': 'Since v = c / n, the speed of light is inversely proportional to refractive index. Water has a lower refractive index (1.33 < 2.42), so light travels significantly faster in water.', 'steps': ['Recall v = c / n: higher n means slower light speed.', 'Compare: n(water) = 1.33, n(diamond) = 2.42.', 'Conclude that light travels faster in water due to its lower refractive index.']}, {'question': 'Why does a lemon kept in water in a glass tumbler appear larger than its actual size when viewed from the sides?', 'options': ['Water chemically reacts with the lemon skin to expand it', 'Refraction of light at the curved glass-water interface magnifies the image', 'The lemon absorbs all blue light wavelengths', 'Total internal reflection duplicates the lemon'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'The curved glass tumbler acts like a magnifying cylindrical lens.', 'explanation': 'Light rays exiting from the denser water-glass medium into air bend away from the normal, creating an enlarged virtual image of the lemon.', 'steps': ['Identify the medium transition: light travels from water/glass (denser) into air (rarer).', 'The rays refract away from the normal at the curved cylindrical boundary.', 'The diverged rays reach the eye, which perceives an enlarged virtual image.']}, {'question': 'A ray of light traveling in water enters obliquely into crown glass (n = 1.52). Does the light ray bend toward or away from the normal?', 'options': ['Away from the normal', 'Toward the normal', 'It continues without any deviation', 'It reflects backward at 90 degrees'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Compare the refractive index of water (1.33) with crown glass (1.52).', 'explanation': 'Crown glass (n = 1.52) is optically denser than water (n = 1.33). When light passes from a rarer to a denser medium, it slows down and bends toward the normal.', 'steps': ['Compare optical densities: n(glass) = 1.52 > n(water) = 1.33.', 'Light enters an optically denser medium from an optically rarer medium.', 'Recall the rule: rarer to denser -> ray bends toward the normal.']}, {'question': 'Where should an illuminated object be placed in front of a convex lens to produce a real image of the same size as the object?', 'options': ['At the principal focus (F1)', 'At twice the focal length (2F1)', 'At infinity', 'Between the optical center and focus'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Check Table 9.4 for symmetric real image formation at 2F2.', 'explanation': 'When an object is placed at twice the focal length (2F1) of a convex lens, refracted rays converge at 2F2 to form a real, inverted image of the same size.', 'steps': ['Recall the 6 image formation scenarios for a convex lens.', 'Placing object at 2F1 yields image at 2F2.', 'Image properties: real, inverted, same size (magnification m = -1).']}, {'question': 'Which of the following optical materials cannot be used to make a functional spherical lens?', 'options': ['Water', 'Glass', 'Clear transparent plastic', 'Clay'], 'answer': 3, 'hint': 'A lens must be made of a transparent optical material.', 'explanation': 'Clay is completely opaque and does not transmit light rays; therefore, it cannot be used to fabricate an optical lens.', 'steps': ['Define a lens: a transparent optical medium bounded by at least one spherical surface.', 'Water, glass, and clear plastic are transparent to visible light.', 'Clay is opaque, absorbing and blocking light, so it cannot form a lens.']}, {'question': 'A ray of light traveling parallel to the principal axis strikes a double concave lens. How does it emerge after refraction?', 'options': ['It passes directly through the second focus F2', 'It appears to diverge from the first principal focus F1', 'It turns back into the incident beam', 'It travels straight down along the normal'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'A concave lens is a diverging lens.', 'explanation': 'In a concave lens, incident rays parallel to the principal axis diverge outward such that their backward extensions appear to emanate from the principal focus F1.', 'steps': ['Identify the lens: double concave (diverging lens).', 'Recall ray tracing rule 1 for concave lenses.', 'Incident parallel rays diverge away, appearing to originate from focus F1.']}, {'question': 'You wish to observe a magnified, upright virtual image of small print in a dictionary. Which lens should you select?', 'options': ['A convex lens of focal length 5 cm', 'A concave lens of focal length 5 cm', 'A convex lens of focal length 50 cm', 'A concave lens of focal length 50 cm'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'A magnifying glass requires a convex lens with short focal length for higher power and magnification.', 'explanation': 'A convex lens of short focal length (f = 5 cm) provides high magnifying power (P = 1/f = 20 D) and produces an enlarged upright virtual image when held close to print.', 'steps': ['Magnification requires a convex lens (concave lenses always diminish images).', 'Shorter focal length gives higher optical power (P = 1/f) and greater angular magnification.', 'Therefore, choose a convex lens with the shorter focal length (5 cm).']}, {'question': 'In an experiment with a rectangular glass slab, if the angle of incidence is 45 degrees, what can be deduced about the angle of emergence?', 'options': ['Angle of emergence is 0 degrees', 'Angle of emergence equals 45 degrees', 'Angle of emergence is 90 degrees', 'Angle of emergence is 22.5 degrees'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'The emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray across parallel slab boundaries.', 'explanation': 'Because opposite faces of the rectangular glass slab are parallel, the angle of emergence strictly equals the angle of incidence: angle e = angle i = 45°.', 'steps': ['Opposite faces of the glass slab are parallel.', 'The deviation at the first interface is canceled by equal and opposite deviation at the second interface.', 'Thus, the angle of emergence e equals the angle of incidence i = 45°.']}, {'question': 'What is the nature of the image formed by a convex lens when an illuminated candle is placed between its optical center (O) and principal focus (F1)?', 'options': ['Real, inverted, and magnified', 'Virtual, erect, and magnified on the same side as the object', 'Real, inverted, and diminished', 'Virtual, inverted, and point-sized'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'This is the classic magnifying glass configuration.', 'explanation': 'When the object distance is less than the focal length (u < f), refracted rays diverge on the opposite side; their backward projections form an erect, magnified virtual image on the same side.', 'steps': ['Object placement: between O and F1 (u < f).', 'Refracted rays emerge diverging, so they cannot intersect to form a real image.', 'Backward ray extensions intersect on the same side of the lens, creating an erect, magnified virtual image.']}]}, {'id': 20, 'title': 'Lens Power Review', 'subtitle': 'Comprehensive Review • Lens Formula, Dioptres, Corrective Eyewear & Net Power', 'speech': '', 'objectives': ['Calculate object and image parameters using 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.', 'Determine optical power of thin lenses using P = 1 / f (in meters).', 'Calculate net optical power for combinations of thin lenses in contact.'], 'key_takeaways': ['Lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f; Lens magnification: m = +v/u.', 'Optical power P = 1/f (in meters); unit is Dioptre (D).', 'Net power of lenses in contact is the algebraic sum: P = P1 + P2 + P3 + ...'], 'questions': [{'question': 'A concave lens has a focal length of 15 cm. At what distance should an object be placed from the lens so that it forms an image at 10 cm from the lens?', 'options': ['-30 cm (30 cm in front of the lens)', '+30 cm (30 cm behind the lens)', '-20 cm', '-50 cm'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Concave lens: f = -15 cm, v = -10 cm. Apply 1/v - 1/u = 1/f.', 'explanation': '1/u = 1/v - 1/f = 1/(-10) - 1/(-15) = -1/10 + 1/15 = -3/30 + 2/30 = -1/30. Thus u = -30 cm (30 cm in front of the lens).', 'steps': ['Assign signs: f = -15 cm, v = -10 cm (concave lens forms virtual image on the same side).', 'Apply lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f => 1/u = 1/v - 1/f = -1/10 - (-1/15) = -1/10 + 1/15.', 'Solve for u: 1/u = -1/30 => u = -30 cm.']}, {'question': 'What is the magnification produced by the concave lens in the previous question (u = -30 cm, v = -10 cm)?', 'options': ['+0.33', '-0.33', '+3.00', '-3.00'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'For lenses, magnification m = +v / u.', 'explanation': 'm = v / u = (-10 cm) / (-30 cm) = +1/3 ≈ +0.33. The positive sign indicates a virtual erect image, one-third the size of the object.', 'steps': ['Given image distance v = -10 cm and object distance u = -30 cm.', 'Apply lens magnification formula: m = +v / u = (-10) / (-30) = +1/3 ≈ +0.33.', 'Positive m confirms the image is virtual, erect, and diminished.']}, {'question': 'A 2.0 cm tall object is placed perpendicular to the principal axis of a convex lens of focal length 10 cm at a distance of 15 cm. Where is the image formed?', 'options': ['+30 cm behind the lens', '-30 cm in front of the lens', '+10 cm', '-15 cm'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convex lens: f = +10 cm, u = -15 cm. Solve 1/v = 1/f + 1/u.', 'explanation': '1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/(+10) + 1/(-15) = 1/10 - 1/15 = 3/30 - 2/30 = 1/30. Therefore, v = +30 cm (30 cm on the other side of the lens).', 'steps': ['Assign signs: h = +2.0 cm, f = +10 cm, u = -15 cm.', 'Apply lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f => 1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/10 - 1/15 = 1/30.', 'Solve for v: v = +30 cm (real image formed 30 cm behind the lens).']}, {'question': 'What is the size and nature of the image formed in the previous question (h = +2.0 cm, u = -15 cm, v = +30 cm)?', 'options': ["h' = -4.0 cm, real and inverted", "h' = +4.0 cm, virtual and erect", "h' = -1.0 cm, real and diminished", "h' = +2.0 cm, virtual and same size"], 'answer': 0, 'hint': "m = v / u = h' / h.", 'explanation': "m = v / u = (+30) / (-15) = -2. Then h' = m * h = -2 * 2.0 cm = -4.0 cm. The negative sign denotes a real, inverted, magnified image.", 'steps': ['Calculate magnification: m = v / u = 30 / (-15) = -2.', "Calculate image height: h' = m × h = -2 × 2.0 cm = -4.0 cm.", 'Conclude that the image is real, inverted, and magnified (4.0 cm tall).']}, {'question': 'Define the SI unit of power of a lens (1 dioptre).', 'options': ['The power of a lens having a focal length of 1 centimeter', 'The power of a lens having a focal length of 1 meter', 'The power of a lens having a focal length of 100 meters', 'The power of a flat glass plate with infinite focal length'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Use P = 1 / f with f = 1 meter.', 'explanation': 'One dioptre is formally defined as the power of a lens whose focal length is exactly 1 meter (1 D = 1 m⁻¹).', 'steps': ['Recall formula: P = 1 / f, where f is in meters.', 'Substitute f = 1 meter: P = 1 / 1 m = 1 m⁻¹ = 1 Dioptre (1 D).', 'Select the 1 meter focal length definition.']}, {'question': 'A doctor prescribes a corrective lens of power -2.5 D. What is the focal length and type of this lens?', 'options': ['f = -40 cm, concave (diverging) lens', 'f = +40 cm, convex (converging) lens', 'f = -25 cm, concave lens', 'f = +25 cm, convex lens'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'f = 1 / P. Negative power means a concave lens.', 'explanation': 'f = 1 / P = 1 / (-2.5 D) = -0.40 m = -40 cm. The negative sign confirms that this is a diverging concave lens (used to correct myopia).', 'steps': ['Given P = -2.5 D.', 'Calculate focal length in meters: f = 1 / P = 1 / (-2.5) = -0.40 m.', 'Convert to centimeters: -0.40 m = -40 cm; negative sign indicates a concave lens.']}, {'question': 'Find the focal length of a lens of power +2.0 D. What type of lens is it?', 'options': ['+0.5 m (+50 cm), convex lens', '-0.5 m (-50 cm), concave lens', '+2.0 m (+200 cm), convex lens', '-2.0 m (-200 cm), concave lens'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'f = 1 / P with P = +2.0 D.', 'explanation': 'f = 1 / P = 1 / (+2.0 D) = +0.5 m = +50 cm. Positive focal length and power designate a converging convex lens (used to correct hypermetropia).', 'steps': ['Given P = +2.0 D.', 'Calculate f = 1 / P = 1 / 2.0 = +0.5 m = +50 cm.', 'Positive power indicates a convex (converging) lens.']}, {'question': 'An optician places two thin lenses in close contact with powers +3.0 D and -1.0 D. What is the net focal length of the combination?', 'options': ['+50 cm (+0.5 m)', '-50 cm (-0.5 m)', '+25 cm (+0.25 m)', '+20 cm (+0.2 m)'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Calculate net power P = P1 + P2, then find f = 1 / P.', 'explanation': 'Total power P = P1 + P2 = +3.0 D + (-1.0 D) = +2.0 D. Net focal length f = 1 / P = 1 / 2.0 = +0.5 m = +50 cm.', 'steps': ['Add individual lens powers: P = P1 + P2 = +3.0 D + (-1.0 D) = +2.0 D.', 'Calculate net focal length: f = 1 / P = 1 / (+2.0) = +0.5 m.', 'Convert to centimeters: +0.5 m = +50 cm.']}, {'question': 'A convex lens of focal length 20 cm is placed in contact with a concave lens of focal length 25 cm. What is the net power of the combination?', 'options': ['+1.0 D', '-1.0 D', '+9.0 D', '-9.0 D'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Convert focal lengths to meters: f1 = +0.20 m, f2 = -0.25 m. Find P1, P2 and sum them.', 'explanation': 'P1 = 1 / (+0.20 m) = +5.0 D; P2 = 1 / (-0.25 m) = -4.0 D. Combined power P = P1 + P2 = +5.0 D - 4.0 D = +1.0 D.', 'steps': ['Find power of convex lens: f1 = +20 cm = +0.2 m => P1 = 1 / 0.2 = +5.0 D.', 'Find power of concave lens: f2 = -25 cm = -0.25 m => P2 = 1 / (-0.25) = -4.0 D.', 'Calculate total power: P = P1 + P2 = +5.0 D + (-4.0 D) = +1.0 D.']}, {'question': 'A student projects the real image of a distant tree onto a white screen using a convex lens. What does the measured distance between the screen and the lens represent?', 'options': ['The diameter of the lens aperture', 'The approximate focal length of the convex lens', 'Twice the focal length of the convex lens', 'The height of the distant tree'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Light from distant objects (infinity) converges at the focal point.', 'explanation': 'Light rays from a distant tree arrive parallel to the principal axis and converge at the principal focus F2 on the screen; therefore, the lens-to-screen distance is the focal length f.', 'steps': ['Distant object is at optical infinity (u ≈ ∞).', 'Refracted parallel rays converge to form a sharp real image at the principal focus F2.', 'The distance between the optical center of the lens and the screen is the focal length f.']}]}]
